#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""dlna-audio-sink - use any DLNA/UPnP MediaRenderer as a system audio output.

Creates a PipeWire/PulseAudio null sink, captures its monitor, encodes the
audio and serves it over HTTP, then tells the renderer (a TV, an AV receiver,
a networked speaker) to play that stream. The renderer then shows up like any
other output device, so a TV's own audio path - including HDMI ARC to an
amplifier - becomes usable from the computer with no cable.

Two behaviours of real renderers drive the design:

* Many hang forever in a "transitioning" state when the HTTP response carries
  no Content-Length, because they try to buffer a file of unknown size. We
  therefore advertise a large bogus length for the endless stream.

* They routinely advertise formats they cannot actually play. So we probe
  candidates in order and keep the first that genuinely reaches PLAYING.

Licensed under the MIT License.
"""

